  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1\n\nA weak cold front will slowly move south of the region today. As\na result, clouds should build in from the north with spotty\nshowers as well. Temperatures will have a wide range as well\nfrom the low 60's to 70's. High pressure then looks to build \ninto the region tonight through Thursday with more seasonable\ntemperatures.\n\nA warm front is still modeled to move into the region Thursday\nnight with enough lift and moisture to develop a fairly\nwidespread round of showers. Influence from a LLJ may keep\ntemperatures fairly steady or lead to slightly rising\ntemperatures overnight. Any portion of the region that gets \ninto the warm sector and has some clearing has the potential to \nsee highs near 70 Friday afternoon. While locations not getting\ninto the warm sector seeing more widespread coverage of showers\nand much cooler temperatures. Uncertainity is fairly high given\na tight gradient of conditions is expected. \n\nAnother round of showers should then sweep through the region\nFriday afternoon and evening along the cold front. Will have to\nwatch for any instability advection that could result in a few \nthunderstorms with gusty winds across NEPA extending\nnortheastward. Total rainfall looks to range from 1/2 to 1 inch,\nnot enough for any flooding issues. \n\nTemperatures should drop sharply Friday night into the 30's.\nClouds and wind should prevent frost formation. Lingering lake \neffect moisture could allow for the precipitation to end as a \nfew snow flurries Saturday morning in CNY. Winds should crank up\nas well with a developing low pressure system tracking north of\nour region with a few gusts near 30 mph potentially. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\n\nHigh pressure then is modeled to build into the region this\nweekend into early next week. The high pressure will move into a\nlocation favorable to give our region a gradual warming trend\nearly next week with highs getting into the 60's again Monday\nand Tuesday.\n\n\nUncertainity is present on how strong the winds will be Saturday\nnight. Clear skies under the high pressure would favor fairly \nwidespread frost with upper 20's and low 30's overnight. \nHowever, winds are currently modeled at 5-10 mph which would \nstill be high enough to limit frost formation.",
